Lovebirds are very happy birds, which express it through a variety of sounds. However, for bird experts they are not considered too singing birds, such as the canary or the Australian parakeet.
Researchers of this species have recorded that when they live in pairs it can be observed how they maintain funny conversations between them of several minutes.
In their natural habitat they are located in flocks, which they sing simultaneously. Listening to them is considered quite a show.
To communicate with their master, they use various sounds; mainly if they do not have a partner and want to give the owner all their love as well as receive his attention.
The specimens that do not adapt quickly tend to manifest a high and repetitive trill; noting that he is alert in his new environment and is uncomfortable.
There are several ways to identify the feelings and states of mind that our Lovebird will show with its song, so below we will explain what the song is according to its state of mind.
The happiness, easy to identify. It is characterized by chirping and singing for hours, showing good spirits and tranquility; show a chirp is low and repetitive.
Aggressiveness it shows when they feel under threat in their cage, it is a dry, short-lived, roaring screech. In this way we can identify that he is on the defensive.
Anxiety and sadness it is one of the most dangerous, because most of these birds are prone to stress and depression. They show it with a low, short and not very repetitive tune.
In the case of young people without a partner, they have to find one to reproduce for the first time, and this they do through their song, which is characterized by a repetitive and powerful chirp.
In addition to emitting its sounds, this little parrot has the gift of imitating the song of different little birds
Those who have the most register of imitating multiple sounds are the Roseicollis and Fischeri; they are the inseparable ones that tend to do this more with the rest of the birds that are in the same cage or habitat.
This way we will prevent them from suffering from stress, anxiety and depression by not having someone with whom to share their life in captivity, which may result in a disease or pathology that strongly damages them.
The male is prone to emit a loud, powerful sound and with roar, apart from being more elongated and sustained according to studies.
In the case of the female, they have a song sharp, melodious and smooth at the same time, lovers of this species say that it is beautiful to hear its tune.
In the female it usually emits a high pitched chirp usually long-lasting, which when threatened can stun if close.
They are parakeets very vocal. The calls are made up of a set of very high-pitched and somewhat loud sounds.
When threatened, its sound is powerful and booming, reproducing long sounds and high frequencies; these parrots do not suffer from as much fear or stress as other species of lovebirds.
While they rarely speak, there is the possibility that they will begin to simulate human speech if taught from a young age. This ability is innate in personatus, and envied by other species.
In the case of the female, her song is sharper than that of the male, with a repetitive but not very loud screech, and if she feels threatened she can sharpen her song, thus managing to stun her if she wants to and at will.
The calls they make on the flight are high pitched and very shrill notes; or jingling at times.
Although for the most part, a sound of harmonic and soft trills can be heard that captivates everyone who hears it.
The taranta female has a melodious and soft sound when she is happy and does not feel at risk, if she is disturbed or stressed, her sounds go from being melodious, to becoming repetitive squeaks.
These beautiful birds have spectacular sounds that surprise and excite, they are considered one of the lovebirds with the most harmonious song of all.
The male makes more intense and lasting emissions, which he raises when he feels in danger.
The males are of powerful noises, in fact they are the inseparable ones with more force in the tune, of long duration and thunderous, that at will it can raise more.
In the case of the male, his sound is powerful but somewhat soft when he is relaxed. Alarmed, the noise intensifies and repeats.
Their calls are weak and somewhat sharp; typically a twitter and a trill. Somewhat abrupt and hissing notes are also heard when they are scared.
In the female pullaria it is different, its song is very soft and sweet, but it becomes a very high-pitched whistle when it is seen in danger.
