It is recognized because it has a yellow color instead of the common greenish coloration normally seen in the purebred of the different lovebird species.
It is somewhat difficult to explain how the lutina mutation occurs in lovebirds, but according to scientists it happens when the eumelanin, a pigment of the bird, it looks reduced by 90%.
In addition to melanin, this mutation may be linked to sexThis is commonly known as 'NSL' which means 'not sex-linked' and 'SL' which stands for 'sex-linked'.
This mutation makes eumelanin colorless, rather than black; therefore the plumage looks yellowish.
This mutation is not very easy to get or breed. Thanks to this, its price is very high compared to pure breeds.
Only the Lilianae carrying the alteration almost never survive. Although it also occurs commonly in the Roseicollis lovebird; This species even has the quality that its lutine versions do not necessarily need the orange coloration to be considered lutine.
It is currently a very difficult parakeet to find. Many of the professionals and those interested in the Lovebirds went so far as to say that these types of mutations do not exist.
Although this did not remain so, since the aviary had an image of the taranta lutino lovebird and they showed it to the public, but currently the image in question has not been found on the internet.
This is where we realize that orange can be an optional color. But to be a lutino lovebird parakeet it must have the essence of its yellowish tone.
Another example is the lovebird with yellow wings with greenish tones and a red face, something incredible since in most lutinos the green disappears completely.
In all three cases the parrot does not necessarily need orange, what is required is that the yellow tones are part of the plumage.
But apart from them several mutations arise, the Lutino being one of them. To know if it happens to our winged friend, just look at his eyes.
Orange-faced lovebird Roseicollis lutino: It occurs thanks to the reproduction of the lutino with another mutated species of roseicollis.
Lovebird Roseicollis lutino with turquoise: This is a curious but beautiful mutation, it is characterized by an albino color on its chest and head, while its wings remain light yellow and its beak and legs are flesh-colored.
It is the second most common species in the universe of the inseparable ones.
Yellow Fischeri lutino lovebird: It is produced thanks to the fact that the eumelin is reduced in its entirety, to 100%. In this case it is yellow with red eyes, its mask is an intense red like its beak.
This type of mutation is often combined with lovebirds.
Although they are not very common, it is capable of possessing different mutations that are well seen and attract lovers of inseparable. One of this is the lutina.
They have the head, eyes and bill of an intense scarlet; its eye ring is white, the wings are yellow with white areas. Its chest and tail are amber and its legs are grayish.
All lutino lovebirds personatus, They have a price of around € 20 if it is a papillero lutino and € 25 if it is a young one or a little older.
