Their ease of raising them gives them a title as important in the world of Lovebirds. You don't need to be a bird expert to own one.
It is not complicated to maintain, requiring little care. More than anything to be accompanied by a couple, to whom they will give their full attention.
During the breeding season it is possible to become violent. But it will only demonstrate this behavior in case of sharing territory with other species, or another Pullarius but of the same genus.
Sometimes it exhibits a fairly repetitive squeak tune, which can signal that it is in a state of alarm. For example, when they see new people or if they repeatedly move their cage.
Although the roseicollis and the fischeri are the most famous, the pullarius species has the virtue of being a gregarious and docile lovebird.
Although he is shy at first, with time and good care he will change his defensive attitude and create an excellent bond of friendship, love and trust with the people around him.
This bird lives in small groups of 15 or 20 specimens outside the breeding season, of course; only rarely have hundreds of birds of this species been seen in mature grain fields.
Its flight is very fast and straight; they screech loudly and sharply. They are found and inhabit mainly on the edge of forests or in lightly wooded areas of savanna up to 1300 meters above sea level.
There have been some records of migrations and flocks of several Agapornis Pullarius that sometimes go down to a wheat or millet field and destroy the road, that is why they are considered pests.
Like the Canus lovebird. This is a fairly cheerful animal that likes to browse everything around it; This of course, if he was born in captivity.
They are not very violent parrots, except when they are in heat or with their partner; They don't like being around other parakeets very much and they become territorial. They like to have somewhere to bathe inside the cage and have their back stroked, which responds with a few affectionate movements of their head.
This bird is calm and sociable, but caution must be exercised during the breeding season in which these inseparable ones, especially the females of the species, become very skittish and nervous. Reaching the point where they can leave the nest with the eggs or chicks.
Characteristics or color of the male Pullarius
The edge of its wing is navy blue at the height of the shoulders, although some of its feathers are wispy.
The legs are a shade similar to concrete, with dark gray nails.
And the adult pullarius (male and female) have colors in common, the most predominant being green on their body and their gray legs.
Its size is approximately 14 cm, its plumage is intense and bright green, its beak is soft red, reaching an almost orange and its face and neck are red.
There is not much information on the characteristics of this subspecies. Its name comes from the fact that it was discovered in Guinea, by Statius Müller in 1776.
It is not advisable to buy round cages, since neither these nor other birds like this type of cage; they are not comfortable in it.
It is essential to have one or two feeders, in addition it fulfills a very important function. We must always be careful with the contact of food with the animal's feces.
Another element is the sticks or rods, necessary so that they can be more comfortable.
The ideal or the most recommended place to place our lovebirds are on the terrace or in a place near a window. Nor should we forget that at night they must be protected from the impending cold.
In addition to the terrace we can also locate the cages for lovebirds in a place that maintains plenty of sunlight and fresh air.
The size of the cages for lovebirds is the most relevant point, since lovebirds are small and require the right size and environment to make them feel comfortable.
That is why the cages for the pullarius must be high and not elongated, to simulate and maintain their way of life before having entered a state of captivity.
The most important thing is the amplitude, with which they fly from one place to another without going through the martyrdom of colliding with the bars or with other companions. The cage should be large and spacious.
You can keep lovebirds in much smaller cages, but we would have to take them out to fly and exercise. The drawback is that taking them out is not recommended for lovebirds that are adapting, as they could escape.
If you are going to raise this inseparable species yourself, it is important to clean and disinfect the cage prior to placing the nest.
It is important that the interior of our cage is soft, with tree leaves for example.
How big should the nest be for these little Pullarius birds?
Ideally, the nest is horizontal, measuring 23 cm wide x 14,5 cm high and 15,5 cm deep, measurements recommended by experts in lovebirds and this species in particular.
To begin the breeding and reproduction of lovebirds at home we must consider and take into account the age of our birds. In this case, according to experts, the fair and appropriate age is between 12 months and a maximum of 6 years of age.
The female of this species must provide moisture to the nest, for this reason, it is important that she can take frequent baths.
At the time they hatch, it is important to maintain the warmest and warmest possible temperatures in the nest, we are talking about 27 ºC.
The mortality rate of captive-born carrots is very high, so it is not recommended for novices to want to reproduce this species.
The inseparable Pullarius have on average a life that lasts between 10 and 18 years, yes it is in freedom. While in captivity it will depend on factors such as the care and hygiene with which they are.
What care should we have so that our Red-headed Inseparable may live for many years?
. Your diet must be according to the recommendations of the experts, if you get to feed more, or less we could subject you to different dangers.
It also influences exposing it to strong odors that harm it, such as car smoke, different aerosols and pesticides, which are very toxic and can make our parakeet seriously ill.
In captivity it can be fed with different seeds, fruits, and vegetables. One recommendation is that it be fed with supplements so that it is in good condition.
For no reason should avocado, pumpkin, milk, meat, soft drinks, chocolates, coffee, parsley and foods that contain a lot of salt be supplied.
Complement for the appearance of parasites, It ensures a good state of health when our birds are infected with a parasite.
Supplement or vitamin for stress in pullarius, The agapornis pullarius is very sensitive to stress, therefore these types of supplements help it to be active and calm in its day to day.
Vitamins like A, help our little bird to have an appetite.
